# [[.Name]]

An Olivares AI **[[.Template]]** connector, generated by
`olivares-connector-new`.

[[- if .IsContentSource]]
It serves documents and ACL references through `sdk.ContentSource` for governed
knowledge ingestion.
[[- else if .IsOutputSink]]
It delivers engine notifications outward through `sdk.OutputConnector`.
[[- else if .IsAgentSurface]]
It gathers agent-runtime facts through `sdk.SourceConnector`, emitting access
edges and findings.
[[- else if .IsModelProvider]]
It gathers model-backend facts through `sdk.SourceConnector`, emitting cost
samples and usage edges.
[[- else]]
It gathers access facts through `sdk.SourceConnector`, emitting edge
observations.
[[- end]]

Declared governance surfaces:
[[- range .Surfaces]]
- `[[.]]`
[[- end]]

The TODOs in `[[.Package]].go` mark where your system's real logic goes.

**License:** this repository is yours, and so is the license choice. The
scaffold seeds `Apache-2.0` SPDX headers — recommended for ecosystem
compatibility with the Apache-2.0 SDK — but it is your decision: put your name
in the `SPDX-FileCopyrightText` lines and add a matching `LICENSE` file.

## Build

```sh
go mod tidy   # first time[[- if .WithPlugin]] (materializes go.sum for the plugin dependency tree)[[- end]]
go build ./...
```

[[- if .SDKPath]]

`go.mod` carries `replace` directives pointing at the local SDK checkout the
scaffold was given; swap them for tagged versions once the first public
`sdk` tags are published.
[[- else]]

Until the first public tags of `github.com/olivaresai/olivares/sdk` are
published, add a `replace` directive in `go.mod` pointing at a local checkout
of the upstream repo (the note in `go.mod` shows the exact lines).
[[- end]]

[[- if .IsModelProvider]]

Model listing/catalog policy is intentionally not implemented here. Model-access
governance is engine-side; this archetype reports provider usage and cost
evidence through `CostSample` plus usage edges.
[[- end]]

## Test

```sh
go test ./...
```

`[[.Package]]_test.go` proves the full lifecycle the engine drives
[[- if .IsContentSource]] (Open → List → Fetch → Close against an in-memory fake
backend)[[- else if .IsOutputSink]] (Open → Notify → Close, capturing the
delivery in memory)[[- else]] (Open → Gather → Close, with an in-memory sink
collecting emitted observations)[[- end]]. Grow it with every real behavior
you add.

Run the standalone boundary check too:

```sh
./scripts/check-boundary.sh
```

A connector must never link the upstream AGPL engine module — only the
Apache-2.0 SDK. The script checks the real `go list -deps` build graph; it is
the same boundary the upstream repo enforces in its CI. Run it in **your** CI
on every push.

## Sign

[[- if .WithPlugin]]

```sh
go build -o [[.BinName]] ./cmd/...
```

The binary serves the connector over gRPC (hashicorp/go-plugin); the engine
launches and supervises it. The connector code is identical in-process and
out-of-process.
[[- else]]

This repo was generated without a plugin main. To ship out-of-process:

1. Add the `sdk/plugin` module to `go.mod` — both the `require` and (until the
   first public tags exist) a `replace` to your local upstream checkout,
   mirroring the `sdk` lines already there.
2. Add `cmd/[[.BinName]]/main.go` calling
   [[- if .IsContentSource]] `plugin.ServeContentSource(New())`[[- else if .IsSource]] `plugin.ServeSource(New())`[[- else]] `plugin.ServeOutput(New())`[[- end]]
   from `github.com/olivaresai/olivares/sdk/plugin`.
3. `go mod tidy && go build -o [[.BinName]] ./cmd/...`.

Or re-run the scaffold with plugin output enabled.
[[- end]]

Operators only admit plugins they can verify. Attest the binary with SLSA
provenance — key-based:

```sh
cosign generate-key-pair
cosign attest-blob --key cosign.key --type slsaprovenance1 \
  --predicate predicate.json --bundle [[.BinName]].sigstore.json [[.BinName]]
```

or keyless (ambient OIDC identity, e.g. your CI — no key to manage):

```sh
cosign attest-blob --type slsaprovenance1 \
  --predicate predicate.json --bundle [[.BinName]].sigstore.json [[.BinName]]
```

`predicate.json` is your SLSA provenance predicate (who built what, from which
commit). Never commit `cosign.key` — `.gitignore` already excludes it.
Alternative: GitHub artifact attestations (`actions/attest-build-provenance`
in your release workflow, verifiable with `gh attestation verify`).

## Register

Either a GitHub release carrying the binary, its sha256 and the
`.sigstore.json` bundle, or an OCI registry:

```sh
oras push ghcr.io/<org>/[[.BinName]]:0.1.0 [[.BinName]] [[.BinName]].sigstore.json
```

[[- if .IsOutputSink]]

Host-side external wiring currently covers observation sources and content
sources. This output connector builds, signs and ships exactly like the others,
but the engine's notify composition does not yet load external output plugins.
[[- else]]

Register the plugin in the operator-owned sources config, pinned by digest and
admitted by the root `connector_trust` policy.
[[- end]]

## Operate

[[- if .IsContentSource]]

The operator wires the plugin into governed knowledge through the `documents`
block in the file named by `OLIVARES_SOURCES_CONFIG`:

```json
{
  "connector_trust": {
    "trusted_keys": ["-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\n<contents of [[.Vendor]]'s cosign.pub>\n-----END PUBLIC KEY-----\n"],
    "allowed_predicates": ["https://slsa.dev/provenance/v1"]
  },
  "documents": [
    {
      "name": "[[.Connector]]",
      "plugin": {
        "path": "/opt/olivares/connectors/[[.BinName]]",
        "sha256": "<hex sha256 of the binary>",
        "bundle": "/opt/olivares/connectors/[[.BinName]].sigstore.json"
      },
      "config": { "base_url": "https://content.example", "token": "store:[[.Connector]]-token" }
    }
  ]
}
```

The knowledge module opens the source after the secret store is available and
then drives `List` and `Fetch` during ingest. If you later implement
`DeltaContentSource`, the plugin transport auto-declares `content.delta`.
[[- else if .IsSource]]

The operator wires the plugin into the engine via the `sources` block in the
file named by `OLIVARES_SOURCES_CONFIG`:

```json
{
  "connector_trust": {
    "trusted_keys": ["-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\n<contents of [[.Vendor]]'s cosign.pub>\n-----END PUBLIC KEY-----\n"],
    "allowed_predicates": ["https://slsa.dev/provenance/v1"]
  },
  "sources": [
    {
      "name": "[[.Connector]]",
      "tenant": "default",
      "plugin": {
        "path": "/opt/olivares/connectors/[[.BinName]]",
        "sha256": "<hex sha256 of the binary>",
        "bundle": "/opt/olivares/connectors/[[.BinName]].sigstore.json"
      },
[[- if .IsAgentSurface]]
      "config": { "endpoint": "https://agent-runtime.example", "log_path": "/var/log/agent-runtime.log" }
[[- else if .IsModelProvider]]
      "config": { "provider_api_base": "https://models.example", "api_key": "store:[[.Connector]]-api-key" }
[[- else]]
      "config": { "endpoint": "https://your-system.example" }
[[- end]]
    }
  ]
}
```
[[- else]]

When external output wiring lands it will reuse the same signed plugin
descriptor and the same `connector_trust` anchor a source uses. Until then,
build and test this connector as a library/plugin artifact, but do not deploy a
host config expecting it to be loaded.
[[- end]]

`trusted_keys` carries the PEM public-key **material** inline (JSON-escaped),
never a file path. For keyless signing the gate verifies against the Fulcio CA
root, so it needs a trust anchor in `trusted_roots` **plus** the identity pins:

```json
"trusted_roots": ["-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\n<Fulcio root PEM>\n-----END CERTIFICATE-----\n"],
"allowed_identities": ["https://github.com/<org>/<repo>/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/*"],
"allowed_issuers": ["https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"]
```

`allowed_identities` and `allowed_issuers` go together (set both or neither);
the gate refuses an enforcing policy that has neither a `trusted_root` nor a
`trusted_key`, so the Fulcio root is mandatory for keyless.

## Governed by construction

> Enforcement is engine-side by construction. A connector never links
> governance code, and a connector cannot opt out.

You write zero governance code in this connector. The engine keys controls on
the configured source identity (`source_type`, `source_ref`), applies scoping,
model access, content controls and audit, and treats `Descriptor.Surfaces` as
catalog/admission metadata only — never an enforcement input.

## Certify

Submit the connector for the **verified-connectors index** so operators can
discover it with its provenance pre-checked: the verified-connectors
reference and the full connector authoring guide are both published at
<https://olivares.ai/docs>.
